{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\": \"submission.json\", \"summary\": \"Submitted draft/submission.json (action=review) for the accepted direct check 22a74b164f184eeba537d581819fa670 (version 1) of obligation c-at-most-one-leader-per-term, the run's strongest finding. It supplies the whole-artifact contract the controller requested — the required checker_correspondence item plus the applicability item — and resolves both open input issues on this artifact's lineage (a025d4c58b334a8b8238edc1e218543c on the claim and 5f1472a8f0804ea1896e98de1b55f7ad on the fixed plan), each with a retained condition disposition of independent scope. The correspondence item (no_issue_found, 9 cited ranges, 5 retained limitations) states the checked proposition and reviews inputs, binding, oracle, correlation, legality and counterevidence together: three voters built by the package's own NewRaft over the real NetworkTransport with a socket-free StreamLayer; two stores seeded with the bootstrap entry and CurrentTerm = 2 as initialisation data; one endpoint blocked at the fabric for exactly one election (message loss); one StableStore CurrentTerm write held inside SetUint64 on the lagging voter (latency on an injected dependency); a ReloadConfig call on the leader that lengthens its own intervals using values read back from ReloadableConfig; an observer filter with a nil channel that reads, synchronously at the leader transition, the target's term and the other node's published state and term through public accessors; an oracle whose applicability restricts judgement to the case where the other node still held Leader in the admitted term and whose assertion requires the two terms to differ, so equality is the violation; and a prerequisite chain with the admission event preceding the result in the single runner stream with matching identity and a later complete result. The retained observation is the violation: first_leader_adopted with d1 Leader at term 3 and both peers following, then second_leader_observed with t1 Leader at term 3, other_node_state Leader, other_node_term 3, witness_recorded true, and final voter states showing both leaders reverting, at exit 0. Counterevidence is kept explicit rather than suppressed: the overlap is transient because the two leaders revert when they exchange traffic, which the instantaneous observer read is designed for and the final states confirm; the seeded terms, blocked endpoint and delayed write are initialisation, message-loss and latency choices on injected dependencies; the ordinary vote decisions stay conservative; the second quorum needs a voter whose record for the term is empty, which the block arranges; and the reading that leadership transfer lies outside the safety argument is retained as an alternative. The applicability item (no_issue_found, 9 cited ranges, 3 limitations) argues why the implementation owes one leader per term — leadership is a quorum plus the node's own claim, and the grant rule implements one vote per term — and answers the transfer-exemption condition as an outstanding-repair boundary that identifies the responsible component and the two repair locations without discharging the duty. With both blockers answered, the assessment for this artifact should recompute to a confirmed violated comparison at implementation_obligation level, completing the obligation and turning its conclusion into a confirmed in-scope consensus-safety result. Optional preflight with the trusted validator returned valid=true; the review introduces no new execution and no map change, and the Chinese fields are presentation only.\"}"}}
